    <GaugingStation location="Scorton Weir" name="Wyre" number="72016">
        <Comments>
            <Comment type="Station Summary">Non-standard weir with small fish pass. Artificial influence from Lune-Wyre transfer via Abbeystead reservoir.</Comment>
            <Comment type="Hydrometric">Siltation of stilling well affects low flow measurement; this was a serious problem in 2009 and Jan 2010. Large floods spill out of bank into gravel pits, bypassing station. The November 1980 floods breached many embankments on the River Wyre, including upstream of Scorton, so that the level at the station in this event may have been less than if the embankments had held. After the January 1995 floods, around 1996, the right bank was raised, and QMED is now roughly within bank. During high flows gravel and boulders are washed over the weir causing erosion to the crest. This erosion and repairs have led to multiple ratings being applied throughout the station record, all similar at high flows, so a single one is adopted. Three peak flow ratings applied across period of record, derived from current meter gaugings, with a modelled top-end to allow for floodplain flow. Current meter gaugings taken at high flows by handline from a nearby footbridge. The rating curve should account for fish pass flow as the flows used to create the rating curve were gauged upstream of the fish pass structure.</Comment>
            <Comment type="General">Station opened in January 1967 with a non-standard compound broad-crested weir and fish pass and closed in 2015.</Comment>
            <Comment type="Flow record">No data from May 1997-May 1998 due to problems at site. Peak flows are shown but marked as rejected before the current raised embankments in 1996. No data after storm Desmond on 05/12/2015. Daily mean flows before 01/10/1976 are not held as they are historically inconsistent with peak flows and with post-1976 daily mean flows. Full period of record peak flow data reviewed and released in August 2025 (WINFAP Files v14).</Comment>
            <Comment type="Flow regime">Abbeystead reservoir in centre of catchment controls approx 50%. Adjacent gravel workings and Lune-Wyre transfer via the reservoir (see 72002) can affect flow regime.</Comment>
            <Comment type="Catchment">A predominantly rural and agricultural catchment with moorland fed headwaters. Geology is almost entirely Millstone Grit. A major geological fault is situated just downstream of the weir. No significant catchment changes.</Comment>
            <Comment type="Factors affecting runoff">P</Comment>
        </Comments>
        <Usages>
            <Usage notes="Gauged above QMED. Good fit to gaugings." suitable="Yes" type="QMED"/>
            <Usage notes="No high flow gaugings. With modelled top-end probably suitable from 1996, but too much uncertainty in the embankment heights and floodplain flow before then." suitable="No" type="Pooling"/>
        </Usages>
    </GaugingStation>
